        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the probiotic before the protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A probiotic is a minor ingredient in a protein powder. If the protein source, sweetener or fiber does not suit you, a few billion bacteria will not fix it. Choose the powder on protein per serving, ingredients, testing and price first, then use the probiotic or enzyme as a tiebreaker between otherwise similar options.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a species name without a dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A label that says Bacillus coagulans or Lactobacillus acidophilus with no CFU count gives you nothing to compare against research. Probiotic effects are tied to specific strains at specific doses. Prefer labels that list a strain code, such as DE111, and a CFU figure, ideally guaranteed through the expiration date rather than at manufacture.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heating or baking a probiotic powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stirring a probiotic powder into boiling coffee or baking it into pancakes can kill fragile Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains. Spore-forming Bacillus strains tolerate heat better, but the manufacturer data covers only a few minutes. If you mostly cook with your protein powder, skip the probiotic version and save the premium.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting enzymes to fix bloating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Added proteases have modest evidence for raising blood amino acids after a shake, not for reducing gas or bloating. If a shake upsets you, look first at lactose, sugar alcohols, inulin and gums. Switching to an isolate or a shorter ingredient list usually helps more than a protein powder with digestive enzymes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping a prescribed probiotic for a shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a doctor recommended a specific probiotic, for example during antibiotics, take that exact product at its stated dose. A protein powder with a different strain, a smaller dose or an undisclosed blend is not an equivalent, and its dose changes every time you change your scoop size or skip a shake.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder with probiotics?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein is our top pick. It lists 30 g of protein and 2 billion CFU of the spore-forming strain Bacillus subtilis DE111 per serving, per the label at the time of writing, and it is NSF Certified for Sport. Vega Performance Protein + Recovery uses the same strain and dose at a higher price per serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do probiotics survive in protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the strain. Spore-forming Bacillus strains such as DE111 and Bacillus coagulans are built to survive dry storage, and DE111's supplier reports 24 months of stability under controlled conditions. Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains are more fragile and lose numbers with heat, moisture and repeated opening. Few powders publish independent stability data inside the finished product.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are digestive enzymes in protein powder worth it?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually only as a minor bonus. Lactase can help people with lactose intolerance in whey concentrate. Proteases have small studies, including a 2024 trial on pea protein, showing higher blood amino acids after a shake, but no clear evidence of more muscle or less bloating. Prefer brands that disclose enzyme doses, and do not pay much extra.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it better to take a separate probiotic?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people with a specific goal, yes. A standalone probiotic lets you choose the strain that was studied for that goal and take the studied dose every day, whether or not you drink a shake. A combined powder fixes the probiotic dose to your protein dose. It suits people who want convenience rather than a targeted result.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does whey protein have probiotics?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plain whey does not contain live probiotics; it is a dairy protein filtered from cheese-making liquid. Some whey products add them, such as Muscle Milk 100% Whey with Probiotics, which retailer listings show with Bacillus subtilis DE111 at 1 billion CFU. Many whey isolates add digestive enzymes instead, including Momentous, Thorne and GHOST.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can probiotic protein powder cause bloating?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some people notice temporary gas or bloating when starting a probiotic, and many probiotic powders also contain inulin, chicory root, gums or sugar alcohols that cause bloating on their own. Start with half a scoop, increase slowly, and change one product at a time. If symptoms persist or are severe, speak to a doctor rather than switching powders repeatedly.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is probiotic protein powder safe for everyone?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most healthy adults tolerate it well, but people who are immunocompromised, critically ill, pregnant, or have a central line or recent surgery should ask a doctor before taking any live probiotic. The same applies if you have kidney disease, allergies or take regular medication. This guide is general information and not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
